WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:08.960 align:start position:0%
For the first time ever, we saw weather
outside our solar system. It's over

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:13.040 align:start position:0%
seven light years away on this weird
freezing world about the size of

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:18.800 align:start position:0%
Jupiter, but over twice its mass. It
floats alone in space without a host

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.960 align:start position:0%
star. Too big to be a planet, but too
small to be a star. So for 11 hours

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:26.400 align:start position:0%
straight, scientists locked the James
Webb Space Telescope on it and just

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.519 align:start position:0%
watched. And every 15 minutes, they
collected a new light spectrum, which

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000 align:start position:0%
let them see the different types of
molecules shifting in the atmosphere. So

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.719 align:start position:0%
they could watch water clouds get
thicker and thinner as it rotated, which

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.719 align:start position:0%
is insane because it would take the
Voyager spacecraft

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:49.920 align:start position:0%
125,000
years to get there. But what if you just

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.920 align:start position:0%
kept going? To see that, subscribe.

